// Build settings for the iOS app. COMMITTED — edit Local.xcconfig, not this.
//
// The pattern, and why it is this shape:
//
//   Base.xcconfig    committed. Holds a working DEFAULT for every setting, so
//                    a fresh clone builds without anyone creating a file.
//   Local.xcconfig   gitignored. Yours. Overrides any of the below.
//
// The `#include?` at the bottom is Xcode's OPTIONAL include — the question
// mark is what makes a missing Local.xcconfig silently fine rather than a
// build error. Referencing the gitignored file directly would break every
// fresh clone, which is the trap this avoids.
//
// Same split as config.yaml / config.example.yaml on the Mac side, and for the
// same reason: nothing describing one machine belongs in the repository.

// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// MACALENDAR_SERVER_URL — where the app looks for your Mac by default
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Reaches Swift as `AppSettings.defaultServerURL`, via the Info.plist key
// `MACalendarServerURL`.
//
// This was a string literal in three Swift files — the Settings help text, a
// comment in APIClient.base, and the error message telling you to go and type
// it in — while the setting itself defaulted to "". So a fresh install could
// name the host it was refusing to call, and sat there reporting "Your Mac
// isn't reachable" about a Mac whose address it was printing on screen.
//
// The placeholder below is deliberately not a real address: unset is honest,
// and Settings then asks for one. Put yours in Local.xcconfig:
//
//     MACALENDAR_SERVER_URL = http://100.x.x.x:8080
//
// NO SCHEME. Write "100.x.x.x:8080", not "http://100.x.x.x:8080".
//
// In an xcconfig `//` STARTS A COMMENT, including inside a value — so
// "http://host:8080" is silently truncated to "http:" and the app ends up
// pointed at nothing. It builds, it installs, and it fails at runtime looking
// exactly like an unreachable Mac. Caught here only by reading the value back
// out of the BUILT Info.plist.
//
// `APIClient.base` prepends http:// anyway, and accepts a bare IP, "host:port"
// or a Tailscale MagicDNS name. Find yours with `tailscale ip -4` on the Mac.
MACALENDAR_SERVER_URL = 

#include? "Local.xcconfig"
